course: dictionary
lesson: 11373
title: Aboard: on a ship, plane, train or bus
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Aboard means on or into a ship, plane, train or bus. When you climb aboard a bus, you get on it. «The children climbed aboard the train at the station.» The clue is climbed and train: they got on the train.
explain: You may also hear «All aboard!» This is a call telling people to get on, because the vehicle is about to leave. «All aboard!» the driver shouted, and the passengers hurried on.
explain: Aboard can also mean joining a team or project, especially in informal talk. «Welcome aboard, Maya!» means welcome to the team. Use the context to decide: is someone getting on a vehicle, or joining a group?
fact: Aboard means on or into a ship, plane, train or bus.
fact: «All aboard!» is a call telling people to get on because the vehicle is leaving.
fact: «Welcome aboard» means welcome to a team or project (informal).
fact: Passengers aboard a bus or train can be counted: if 8 are aboard and 3 get off, 5 are still aboard.
exercise: «The children climbed aboard the bus.» Where did the children go? | Away from the school | Under the bus | Past the bus | *Onto the bus
exercise: «All aboard!» the guard called. What is the guard telling people to do? | Get off the train | Sing a song | Wait until tomorrow | *Get on, because the train is leaving
exercise: 8 passengers are aboard a bus. 3 get off at the next stop. How many are still aboard? | 8 | 3 | *5 | 11
test: «Welcome aboard, Sam!» says the team leader. What does aboard mean here? | Sleeping on a train | Lost at sea | *Joining the team | Standing on a boat
test: Which phrase means getting on a vehicle? | Look around | *Climb aboard | Sit down quietly | Fall asleep
exam: Which sentence uses aboard to mean on a plane? | *The pilot welcomed us aboard the plane | We walked past the shop | The sun was hot | The cat sat on the mat
